# Technique: a camera that is a curve of time

[Motion techniques](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques) · Reviewed November 16, 2026

Our own Tabletop Miniature style (approved on October 1, 2026) is one small 3D model on a dark table, rendered in real time with Three.js and exported through HyperFrames. There are no cuts: one orbiting camera flies from station to station. The camera is a pure function of time, built from keyframed channels.

[Figure, animated on the site: One camera channel, the azimuth, keyed at four stations with two holds. Dashed: linear keys (corners). Orange: a Catmull-Rom spline (dips below the first key, rises above the second). Blue: monotone Hermite. On the right the monotone curve drives the camera around the model.]

## How it works

The rig has four numbers: a target point, an azimuth (angle around the model), an elevation and a distance. Every channel is a list of keys joined by curves; the style also keys its post-processing values (blur band, blur radius, depth aperture) in the same table. At time `t` the renderer reads each channel and places the camera. Nothing else moves it.

The choice of curve matters. A **linear** join has a corner at every key: the camera starts and stops abruptly. A general **spline** is smooth but can overshoot: next to a hold it backs up before moving, or swings past the station. A **monotone cubic Hermite** curve (Fritsch and Carlson) picks its tangents so that the curve never leaves the range of the two keys around it, and has a zero slope on a hold ([Wikipedia: monotone cubic interpolation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monotone_cubic_interpolation)). A hold in the keys is a real hold in the picture.

## How to build it

```js
function monotone(xs, ys) {                      // Fritsch-Carlson tangents + cubic Hermite
  const d = xs.slice(1).map((x, i) => (ys[i + 1] - ys[i]) / (x - xs[i]));
  const m = [d[0], ...d.slice(1).map((v, i) => (d[i] * v <= 0 ? 0 : (d[i] + v) / 2)), d.at(-1)];
  d.forEach((di, i) => {
    if (di === 0) { m[i] = m[i + 1] = 0; return; }
    const a = m[i] / di, b = m[i + 1] / di, s = a * a + b * b;
    if (s > 9) { m[i] = (3 / Math.sqrt(s)) * a * di; m[i + 1] = (3 / Math.sqrt(s)) * b * di; }
  });
  return (x) => hermite(xs, ys, m, x);           // standard cubic Hermite basis on the segment
}
const az = monotone(T, AZ), el = monotone(T, EL), dist = monotone(T, DIST);
const camera = (t) => placeOnSphere(target(t), az(t), el(t), dist(t));   // the only place the camera moves
```

Key the angle as an unwrapped number (350 degrees then 380, not 350 then 20), or the camera spins the long way round. Render `camera(frame / fps)` for every frame.

## Watch for

- **Sight lines at every key.** The style page lists it as a trap: a tall prop between the camera path and a station hides it. Check the view at each key, not only the model.
- **Things with a front.** Props with a front and a back must face the side the camera passes; check from the camera azimuth.
- **Anchored labels.** Tags fixed in 3D can land under the headline at some angles; clamp their screen position.
- **Glare.** A bright surface seen top-down can look milky: lower exposure at those keys.
- **One clock.** Overlays on the page read the same `t`, so they stay with the camera; see [seekable frame](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques/seekable-frame).

**Sources:** our Tabletop Miniature style page (built with Three.js r181 and HyperFrames, one video made from it); the curve is the standard monotone cubic interpolation. The demo draws all three curves from the same keys. The code above is a sketch we have not run.

# Technique: closed-form springs

[Motion techniques](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques) · Reviewed November 16, 2026

The prompts that ask for a premium UI feel agree on how things should move: with springs, and with numbers, not adjectives. One prompt gives them: the shape uses damping 0.72 and settles in 0.35 s, the camera uses damping 1.0 and settles in 0.6 s ([flxrnc](https://x.com/flxrnc/status/2108242454105251921)). It also asks for the inputs "in hex codes, seconds, damping ratios and pixel sizes instead of adjectives". Two other prompts ask for "springs everywhere, a tiny overshoot at most" ([AnnaCher](https://x.com/AnnaCher___/status/2103571098638152174), [0xnicc0](https://x.com/0xnicc0/status/2107217691505516612)).

[Figure, animated on the site: Both curves are exact formulas, replayed here three times slower than real time. The shape (blue) overshoots by 3.8 % and settles in 0.35 s. The camera (orange) does not overshoot and arrives at 0.6 s.]

## How it works

A spring does not need a physics loop. Its response to a step has a closed form, so the value at time `t` is one expression. That keeps the animation a [pure function of time](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques/seekable-frame): seeking to any moment needs no simulation of the moments before it.

The two numbers mean what they say. **Damping** below 1 overshoots a little and returns; at 1 the value arrives without overshoot. **Settle time** is how long until it stays within 2 % of the target. In the demo, 0.72 gives about 3.8 % overshoot, which reads as life without looking like a cartoon.

Give the camera a slower, non-overshooting spring than the shape. The shape arrives first, then the frame comes to rest around it, and the eye follows the object before the view settles.

## How to build it

```js
// step response, 0 to 1. zeta = damping ratio, settle = seconds to stay within 2 %
function spring(t, zeta, settle) {
  if (t <= 0) return 0;
  if (zeta >= 1) { const w = 5.83 / settle; return 1 - (1 + w * t) * Math.exp(-w * t); }
  const w = 4 / (zeta * settle), r = Math.sqrt(1 - zeta * zeta), wd = w * r;
  return 1 - Math.exp(-zeta * w * t) * (Math.cos(wd * t) + (zeta / r) * Math.sin(wd * t));
}

// one spring per change of target, summed, so the value stays a function of t
const value = changes.reduce((v, c) => v + (c.to - c.from) * spring(t - c.at, 0.72, 0.35), start);
```

Both prompts that animate a main shape ask for this: "when a value changes target several times, add one spring per change" ([0xnicc0](https://x.com/0xnicc0/status/2107217691505516612)).

## Watch for

- **Bounce.** Damping near 0.3 looks like rubber. The prompts keep overshoot small and ban "bouncy cartoon easing".
- **One spring for everything.** Give shape, camera and text their own numbers, or the scene moves as one lump.
- **Easing curves for the camera.** A cubic ease has no memory of an earlier move; a spring per change blends them.

**Sources:** [flxrnc-251921](https://x.com/flxrnc/status/2108242454105251921), [annacher-152174](https://x.com/AnnaCher___/status/2103571098638152174), [0xnicc0-516612](https://x.com/0xnicc0/status/2107217691505516612) in our prompt database. The numbers 0.72, 0.35, 1.0 and 0.6 are the prompt's; the formula and the 3.8 % figure are ours.

# Technique: motion blur from subframes

[Motion techniques](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques) · Reviewed November 16, 2026

Code-drawn motion is razor sharp: a ball that moves 60 px between frames is two crisp discs, and the eye sees a hop. Two prompts in our database ask for real motion blur and say how: render 4 subframes for each frame ([flxrnc](https://x.com/flxrnc/status/2108242454105251921)); "render 4 subframes per frame, blend (tmix-style)" ([techhalla](https://x.com/techhalla/status/2103411244468498547)).

[Figure, animated on the site: The same ball moving fast. Left: one sample per frame. Right: four samples blended, which leaves a short streak behind the ball.]

## How it works

A camera exposes for a part of the frame time, so a moving object smears. A coded scene can fake this by evaluating the picture at several moments inside one frame and averaging. At 60 fps a frame is 1/60 s; with four subframes the picture is drawn at `t`, `t + 1/4`, `t + 1/2` and `t + 3/4` of that interval and blended into one frame. It works because the picture is a [function of time](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques/seekable-frame): asking for a time between frames costs nothing.

## How to build it

```js
const SUB = 4, FPS = 60;
for (let f = 0; f < frames; f++) {
  clearAccumulator();
  for (let s = 0; s < SUB; s++) {
    renderAt(f / FPS + (s / SUB) / FPS);
    accumulate(canvas, 1 / SUB);     // add this sample with weight 1/4
  }
  writeFrame(accumulator);
}
```

Rendering is four times slower, so keep it for the final render and test with one sample ([render and inspect](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques/render-and-inspect)).

## Watch for

- **Blur on text that should be crisp.** Fast text can smear into mush; blur the moving layer, not a settled label.
- **Too few subframes on very fast moves.** Four samples show visible steps when an object crosses most of the frame in one frame time; add samples or slow the move.
- **Blur and a [stable seed](https://production-warehouse.suenot.com/#/techniques/cadence-and-seed).** Sample times between frames must not change the seed of a hand-drawn wobble, or the subframes disagree.

**Sources:** [flxrnc-251921](https://x.com/flxrnc/status/2108242454105251921), [techhalla-498547](https://x.com/techhalla/status/2103411244468498547) in our prompt database. The accumulation loop is ours.
